7:Selected Tak–Dutta–Nath, *Minimum second neighborhood degree energy of commuting graphs of finite rings*, arXiv:2605.22160v1, submitted 21 May 2026, Problem 5.2. Actual source HTML and latest abstract/history retrieved 29 October 2026, 05:40 UTC; only v1 is listed. Primary URL: https://arxiv.org/html/2605.22160v1 . Read the definitions, equations (1)–(2), Section 5 theorem/proof bodies and final problems, not merely the abstract.
9:Problem 5.2 asks for examples of finite noncommutative rings with MSN-hyperenergetic commuting graph, AND a characterization of all such finite rings. Both clauses matter. The example construction below is not a solution of the complete problem, not a refutation of a universal assertion made by its authors, and not a nominated discovery. The strongest objection is both incomplete scope and routine classical spectral reduction. There is no specific MathDB ID claimed for Problem 5.2.
11:The commuting graph has vertices R minus Z(R), with two DISTINCT vertices adjacent precisely when they commute. For a vertex x, the source uses N²(x)=(union over y in N(x) of N(y)) minus {x}. This includes neighbors in triangles; it is NOT the set of vertices at distance exactly two. Put delta₂(x)=sum of the ordinary degrees of vertices in N²(x). The MSN matrix has off-diagonal entry min(delta₂(x),delta₂(y)) on an edge, and zero elsewhere. Its energy is the sum of absolute eigenvalues. MSN-hyperenergetic means this energy exceeds 2(n−1)³, the source's complete-graph benchmark on n vertices.
35:For either ring, noncentral vertices are indexed by u!=0 and t central labels, where t=2 for A_m and t=4 for U_m. Adjacency between different u,v is F(u,v)=0, and each set of t vertices over one u is a clique. The commuting graph is the clique extension of the classical graph H_m=Sp(2m,2) by t, with adjacency
